Title: The pause checkbox reports paused while the sitter keeps admitting
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T11:41Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

**This blocks the release.** `bench/sitter_acceptance.py` FAILS on 0.4.26, built
from `main` at `45da9ac9`, at step 3 — the step whose whole purpose is to prove
the pause is real:

```
11:37:56 acceptance payload .../sitter-acceptance.xpi
11:37:56 acceptance fixture: [4 PDFs]
11:38:02 switch before:    state: 'Indexing is on. Nothing to index right now.'
11:38:06 switch after off: state: 'Indexing is off. Zotero will still index PDFs as you view them.'
11:38:07 holding 30.0s with work outstanding
FAIL: the sitter kept admitting work while paused: completed went 0 -> 4 over 30.0s
```

The fixture is four documents and **all four completed while the control read
"Indexing is off"**. This is the failure the acceptance script's own docstring
names: *"A pause that only reports itself paused is the assertion this repository
keeps finding."*

It is a user-facing lie, not merely a test failure. The author checks a box
labelled "Pause indexing", the panel confirms indexing is off, and the machine
keeps working.

## What is already ruled out

The obvious polarity bug is **not** it, and the obvious missing call is not it
either. Read on `main` at `45da9ac9`:

- `toggle.addEventListener('change', () => toggleSDTSwitch(toggle.checked))` —
  and `.click()` on a checkbox does fire `change`.
- `toggleSDTSwitch(paused)` treats its argument as *paused*, so checked →
  `pausing` → `disarmSDTSitter()`. Polarity is right.
- `disarmSDTSitter()` burns `++sweepGeneration`, clears all three timers, calls
  `sitter?.stop()`, sets `phase = 'switched-off'`.
- `stop()` in `scheduler.js` sets `state.enabled = false` and `epoch++`; the
  loops consult `current() = state.enabled && token === epoch`.

Every link looks correct, which is exactly why this needs an isolation rather
than a guess. The state text tracked the change correctly, so the *display* saw
the stop; the admission path did not.

**The standing suspect, not a conclusion.** `scheduler.js` carries its own
comment near the admission-to-settlement window: *"No `enabled` checks from here
to this document's own settlement"*, with the claim that *"OUTER loops above and
below this one still gate on `enabled`"*. Four completions in thirty seconds is
more than one in-flight document finishing gracefully, so either an outer loop
is not gating as that comment asserts, or admission is reachable by a path that
never consults `current()`. Establish which before changing anything.

## Regression window

The recorded acceptance PASS is **0.4.15**
(`verification/acceptance/0.4.15-2026-09-14.json`), which predates the pause
checkbox. 0.4.26 FAILs. So the regression is somewhere in 0.4.16–0.4.26, and
**0797 rewrote exactly this control** — that makes it the first place to look
and not yet the culprit. Bisect the acceptance across the span rather than
assuming; the span also contains 0796's drain bound, which changed loop
structure.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `stop()`, the `current()` gate, and the
  admission-to-settlement window that documents its own missing checks.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `toggleSDTSwitch`, `disarmSDTSitter`, and
  the checkbox's `change` listener.
- `bench/sitter_acceptance.py` — step 3, which is the reproduction.

## Actions

1. Reproduce headlessly and cheaply: a scheduler-level test that arms the
   sitter with a multi-document backlog, calls `stop()` mid-sweep, and asserts
   **no further admission** — not merely that the outer loop exits.
2. Isolate which loop re-enters admission after `stop()`. Instrument, do not
   reason: every link above reads correct and one of them is not.
3. Fix, and state in the commit which of the two hypotheses held.
4. Re-run the full acceptance on the fixed build and record the JSON under
   `verification/acceptance/`.

## Test

Red first. The test must fail on `45da9ac9` for the reason in the FAIL line —
admission continuing after the stop — and not merely because a loop kept
spinning. Assert on the count of documents ADMITTED after `stop()`, which must
be zero, and let one already-handed-off document settle: that in-flight grace is
the shipped design (`switch-off-in-flight`), not part of the defect.

## Verification

- [ ] The new scheduler test fails on `45da9ac9` and passes after the fix.
- [ ] `bench/sitter_acceptance.py --work-dir <fresh>` reaches step 9 and exits 0.
- [ ] Its JSON lands in `verification/acceptance/` naming the version tested.
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- The in-flight document may still settle after a pause — `switch-off-in-flight`
  promises exactly that, and removing it would be a different, unruled change.
- 0796's drain bound stays bounded; this fix must not restore an unbounded loop.
- The checkbox writes no preference (0797's ruling): nothing here may reintroduce
  remembered state.

## Exit criteria

- A paused sitter admits nothing, demonstrated by a red-then-green scheduler test
  and by a full acceptance run that reaches the end.
- The recorded acceptance PASS names the version that will actually ship.
